+# RCSP modular-order path generator (arXiv:2511.22914)
+
+## Profile and trust status
+
+| Field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | logic |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple (an ordered move list) |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: recognize a modular arithmetic progression among the endpoint-difference variables |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+The generator, exact verifier, and all local gates pass. The required cross-vendor oracle claim is **not yet established**: on 2026-09-05 `scripts/harden.py` made four redraws and every OpenRouter request returned HTTP 403, “Key limit exceeded (total limit).” The harness correctly counted none of those errors as model failures. Thus the module is usable and locally verified, but submission remains blocked until the bare and G9 oracle runs can be repeated with a funded key.
+
+## Problem family
+
+The source is Kei Kimura, [“Towards an algebraic approach to the reconfiguration CSP”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.22914), v3. Definition 2.8 makes satisfying assignments the vertices of a solution graph, joining two assignments exactly when their Hamming distance is one; Definition 2.9 asks whether two supplied solutions are connected.
+
+An instance here is a Boolean CSP over the native relation `LEQ = {(0,0),(0,1),(1,1)}`. It supplies pins, inequalities `x_u <= x_v`, and start/target solutions. The witness is the order in which every endpoint-difference variable changes directly to its target value. `verify` replays that list and checks the affected inequalities exactly. Every such list has the Hamming lower-bound length, so an accepted list is a shortest path.
+
+Generation is inverse: it samples a short modular progression first, uses it as the move order, installs the consecutive inequalities that force that order, and only then samples path-preserving decoy inequalities. It never solves the completed instance to obtain `answer`.
+
+## Why Track B, not Track A
+
+Lemma 3.13 shows that min-closed relations are preserved by an ordered partial Maltsev operation. Lemma 3.7 gives each solution-graph component a unique local minimum and a monotone path to it. Theorem 3.8 consequently solves this RCSP regime by greedy descent in `O(n^2 m |D|^2)` time. More broadly, Theorem 2.35 puts every safely tight Boolean language in P and reserves PSPACE-completeness for the other languages. This family deliberately lies on the tractable side, so claiming Track A would be false.
+
+For these binary inequalities, the strongest reference route is even simpler: extract precedence constraints and run Kahn topological elimination in `O(n+m)`. At the hard preset it used **4,051 counted exact operations** and about **0.005 s**, solving 8/8 audit instances as expected. The compact route notices that the twelve changing indices form a modular arithmetic progression, finds its maximum-overlap translation, and uses one native inequality to orient it; this took **166 counted exact operations**. The benchmark claim is the compression from scanning thousands of displayed constraints to spotting that invariant, not computational intractability.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=7`)
+
+This is the complete hand-scale rendered instance (the syntax example is only a format example):
+
+```text
+Shortest path in a Boolean reconfiguration CSP
+
+There are 11 Boolean variables x0,...,x10;
+their values are 0 or 1 with the usual order 0 < 1.  A pin xj=c requires xj
+to equal c.  A displayed row
+
+    xu <= xv, xw
+
+abbreviates the two constraints xu <= xv and xu <= xw.  An assignment is a
+solution exactly when it obeys every pin and every displayed inequality.
+
+The solution graph has one vertex for every solution.  Two solutions are
+adjacent exactly when they differ in the value of one variable.  A
+reconfiguration path is a sequence of such single-variable changes in which
+every intermediate assignment, including both endpoints, is a solution.
+
+Pins at 0:
+[3, 4, 6, 8, 10]
+Pins at 1:
+[0, 7]
+
+The start assignment has value 1 exactly at these 0-indexed variables and 0
+at all others:
+[0, 1, 2, 5, 7, 9]
+
+The target assignment has value 1 exactly at these 0-indexed variables and 0
+at all others:
+[0, 7]
+
+Inequality rows (row order and the order after <= carry no meaning):
+  x8 <= x6, x4
+  x3 <= x10, x2
+  x2 <= x1, x7, x9
+  x5 <= x1
+  x4 <= x6, x3
+  x9 <= x5
+  x1 <= x0
+  x0 <= x7
+
+Output a shortest path using exactly 4 changes.  Write a
+JSON list [v0,...,v3] of distinct 0-indexed variable
+indices.  Starting at the start assignment, step i changes x_vi directly to
+its target value.  Every listed variable must be one on which the endpoints
+differ, every such variable must occur once, and every intermediate assignment
+must satisfy all pins and inequalities.  Order matters; repeats are forbidden.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as one JSON list of
+exactly 4 integers.
+Example of the required syntax and length (not a claimed solution):
+<answer>[0, 1, 2, 3]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>[2, 9, 5, 1]</answer>` and `verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Swapping the first two moves to `[9, 2, 5, 1]` returns `(False, "step 1 violates inequality x2 <= x9")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by following the three active precedence inequalities.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | Requested variables | Moving variables | Decoy percentage | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 11 | 4 | 12 | hand-solvable illustration |
+| easy | 37 | 10 | 18 | first oracle rung; run blocked by API errors |
+| medium | 73 | 12 | 24 | not reached |
+| hard | 131 | 12 | 32 | configured shipping preset; oracle verdict pending |
+
+Difficulty grows mainly by enlarging and crowding the constraint haystack while the answer remains twelve integers.
+
+## Exact/local gates
+
+| Gate | Result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 planted verifies | pass, 12/12 preset/seed combinations |
+| G2 corruption rejected | pass, 5/5 with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 parse round-trip | pass |
+| G4 structure-aware guessing | pass, 0/200,000; exactly `1/12! = 2.08767569878681e-9` by construction |
+| G5 density and baseline | pass; demo exact count 1; hard reference 4,051 ops; compact route 166 ops |
+| G6 adversary panel | pass; five attacks, each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 scaling | pass; 131 to 263 variables, fixed 12-move answer; reference cost 4,051 to 16,417 ops |
+| G8 canonical key | pass; 160/160 symmetry/carried-witness checks and 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) caps | pass; 60 characters, about 15 tokens, 12 atoms, 166 intended operations |
+
+The five failing attacks are shuffled row position, total-degree outlier, static constraint-score greedy, smallest-gap affine ansatz, and 256 random permutation restarts. The successful standard topological algorithm is reported separately, as Track B requires.
+
+## Oracle and G9 diagnostics
+
+| Run | Preset | Valid attempts | Solved | Infrastructure errors | Conclusion |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| bare ladder | easy | 0 | 0 | 4 | no hardness verdict; HTTP 403 on all redraws |
+| structural hint | hard | 0 | 0 | 4 | diagnostic unavailable |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0 | 0 | 4 | diagnostic unavailable |
+
+The stored `hinted_minus_placebo` value is `0.0` only because both denominators are zero; it is not evidence that the hint had no effect. The structural hint names only the modular-progression invariant. The answer and intended route remain within G9(c)'s 2,000-character, 256-atom, and 300-operation caps.
+
+## Usage
+
+```python
+from gen_2511_22914 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+print(render(inst))
+candidate = parse_answer("<answer>[2, 9, 5, 1]</answer>")
+assert verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit samples after the oracle blocker is cleared with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2511.22914 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is not a complexity-hard distribution. A script can solve it quickly by filtering to endpoint-difference variables and topologically sorting the induced precedence DAG; that is the declared Track B reference algorithm.
+- The exact `1/12!` guessing probability is for the declared structure-aware prior: a uniform random permutation of all endpoint-difference variables. It says nothing about a solver using constraint information or recognizing the modular plant.
+- The modular progression is deliberately visible in the variable labels. That is the intended invariant, but a sufficiently strong model may notice it immediately; only a completed bare oracle run can measure that risk.
+- Plants and decoys are all legal LEQ inequalities, but consecutive plant edges are forced while other path-preserving inequalities are sampled. The panel found no degree/order signature across its eight seeds; it is not a proof against every construction-aware statistic.
+- No external SAT/SMT package was tested. The stronger specialized `O(n+m)` exact topological algorithm was implemented and measured instead. Canonicalization uses one round of directed Weisfeiler–Leman refinement; on a rare unresolved color class it is a strong invariant, not a complete graph-isomorphism canonizer.
