	Additionally, it has been established that $H$-recoloring is solvable in polynomial time when $H$ is a circular clique $C_{p,q}$ for $2 \le p/q <4$ \citep{BMMN16} and
	$H$ is a transitive tournament $\overrightarrow{K_n}$ for $n \ge 3$ \citep{DS23}. 
	Here, for integers $p>q >0$ with $p/q \ge 2$, the \emph{circular clique} $C_{p,q}$ is defined as the graph with vertex set $\{0,1,\dots, p-1\}$ and edge set $\{ ij \mid q \le |i-j| \le p-q \}$.
	%Moreover, a \emph{tournament} is a digraph with exactly one edge between each two vertices, i.e., it is an orientation of an undirected complete graph.
	%Let $\overrightarrow{K_n}$ denote the \emph{standard $n$-tournament}, the digraph with vertex set $[n]$ and with edges $(i,j)$ for all $i < j$.
	%Note that this is the unique \emph{acyclic} tournament on $n$ vertices up to isomorphism, and is sometimes called the \emph{transitive tournament}.
	Moreover, for an integer $n \ge 3$, the \emph{transitive tournament} $\overrightarrow{K_n}$ is defined as the digraph with vertex set $\{0,1,\dots, n-1\}$ and arc set $\{ (i,j) \mid i < j \}$.
	While these graphs do not meet the assumptions of \Cref{thm:LMS25-1,thm:LMS25-2}, \citet{LMS25} demonstrated that H-recoloring for such graphs can nevertheless be addressed through topological techniques.
	
	We show that among the circular cliques $C_{p,q}$ satisfying $2 \le p/q <4$, some are invariant under an ordered partial Maltsev operation while others are not.
	
